## Layout

```
src/{{ package_name }}/
├── models.py     # pydantic domain models — the typed contract this package exports
├── rules.py      # composable, named business-rule predicates
{% if include_cli -%}
├── cli.py        # typer CLI ({{ project_name }} <command>)
{% endif -%}
└── py.typed      # marks the package as typed for downstream mypy
tests/
```

`models.py` ships an `ExampleRecord` showing the house patterns: frozen value
objects, field-level constraints, enums for closed vocabularies. Replace it
with your real entities and keep the patterns.

`rules.py` is a deliberately small rule engine for domains such as underwriting:
rules are named predicates, evaluation returns all violations, and policy rules
can be added without editing a central conditional. Use direct functions when
the rules are simple; the abstraction earns its place only when extension and
explainability matter.

